.he "test (1)                    10/20/26                    test (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
test, [
- condition evaluation
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
test
expression
.br
.bd
[
expression
]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Test evaluates an expression and returns a zero exit status if it is
true, one if it is false, and 255 if the expression is malformed.
With no expression at all it is false.  Bracket is the same program
under a second name and requires the closing bracket as its last
argument.
.sp
The primaries are:
.sp
-r file
.br
true if the file exists and is readable.
.sp
-w file
.br
true if the file exists and is writable.
.sp
-d file
.br
true if the file is a directory.
.sp
-f file
.br
true if the file exists and is not a directory.
.sp
-s file
.br
true if the file exists and has a size greater than zero.
.sp
-t [fd]
.br
true if the descriptor, one if it is omitted, is a terminal.
.sp
-n string
.br
true if the string is not empty.
.sp
-z string
.br
true if the string is empty.
.sp
s1 = s2
.br
true if the strings are identical.
.sp
s1 != s2
.br
true if the strings differ.
.sp
-l s1 op s2
.br
compares the lengths of the two strings rather than their values.
.sp
n1 op n2
.br
compares two integers, where op is one of -eq, -ne, -gt, -ge, -lt
and -le.
.sp
The operators ! (not), -a (and), -o (or) and parentheses combine
primaries.  They are separate arguments, so the shell must see them
as words: quote the parentheses.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
if test -f /etc/passwd -a -r /etc/passwd
.br
then echo readable
.br
fi
.sp
if [ -d /tmp ]
.br
then echo yes
.br
fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Test and bracket are two copies of one binary.  Micronix has no
symbolic links, and a hard link does not survive a tar(1) made here,
so the second name is installed as a copy.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
